bution_function_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does a CDF record?","options":["Only the probability at exactly one point.","The order of outcomes in the sample space","How much probability has accumulated up to a point","The number of independent variables"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"db74fcd66bc545bab6549855"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which monotonicity property does a CDF have?","options":["It is strictly decreasing","It oscillates between negative values","It is always constant at zero","It is nondecreasing"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c361146e26c513f140b50e17"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What are the limiting values of a CDF at infinity?","options":["Both limits are always $1/2$","$\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}F_X(x)=0$ and $\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}F_X(x)=1$","The left limit is $1$ and the right limit is $0$","Both limits are negative"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4f9cb78284a1ba6d79485cf4"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a waiting time $T$, what does $F_T(10)$ mean?","options":["The probability that the wait is exactly negative ten minutes","The largest possible waiting time","The probability that the wait is at most ten minutes","The probability that the wait is exactly ten minutes"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4079a3b81e0d3384c6d0e5cc"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $F_X(3)=0.7$, what probability is being stated?","options":["$P(X=3)=0.7$ always","$P(X\\le3)=0.7$","$P(X>3)=0.7$ always","$P(X\\ge3)=0.3$ always"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"36337969408f2000af061b2b"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a die roll $X$, why does the CDF jump at die values?","options":["Probability mass sits at the possible die values","The die values are impossible","The CDF must decrease at every integer.","The sample space is continuous"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8eb27c2c1566c4e54716da2e"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For a continuous uniform variable on an interval, how does the CDF behave across the interval?","options":["It jumps only at one point","It is negative throughout","It increases linearly","It is undefined"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5dd10c4ce0c3523ae9616bf8"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $X$ always equals $5$, what is $F_X(4)$?","options":["$1$","$5$","$4$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"de410d22efcafff6e2dead03"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does a CDF uniquely determine the distribution?","options":["It encodes all threshold-event probabilities","It gives only the expected value","It lists only impossible outcomes.","It ignores all value ranges"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7494da4cd54983d42c7f0cd2"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does right-continuity mean for a CDF?","options":["The CDF must be symmetric around zero.","The CDF is always differentiable","The CDF decreases at every jump","The value at a point equals the right-hand limit"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5744c832befec2e7bc847870"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why can a CDF have jumps for a discrete random variable?","options":["Discrete variables have no probabilities","Point masses add probability at individual values","A CDF must be continuous for every variable","Jumps represent negative mass"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"72af7922ad7402260348102c"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $X$ is a fair die roll, what is $F_X(3)$?","options":["$3/6$","$1/6$","$4/6$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4aabf66fd38ea274303993a0"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $X$ is a fair die roll, what is $F_X(0)$?","options":["$1/6$","$1$","$6$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5289c4ad109bd2734d29fe96"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $X$ is uniform on $[0,1]$, which CDF value is consistent with linear increase across the interval?","options":["$F_X(0.25)=1$","$F_X(0.25)=-0.25$","$F_X(0.25)=0.25$","$F_X(0.25)=2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"68b214809ca0fdd4383e7b6c"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why must a CDF be nondecreasing?","options":["Probabilities become negative as $x$ increases","The support must be finite","Right-continuity forces every value to be one","The events $\\{X\\le x\\}$ grow as $x$ increases"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4453cafa0bd70049a11a59a0"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What would be wrong with a proposed CDF that decreases from $0.8$ to $0.6$ as $x$ increases?","options":["It violates only the notation for random variables","It is valid for every continuous distribution","It proves the variable is discrete","It violates the nondecreasing property"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"44aa3c3dc6c74d6ac00acf39"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $F_X(2)=0.4$ and $F_X(5)=0.9$, what is $P(2<X\\le5)$?","options":["$1.3$","$0.4$","$0.5$","$0.9$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3cb9930d582b0f22410cb32b"},{"id":"pt_batch5_cumulative_distribution_function_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A proposed CDF has $\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}F_X(x)=0.8$. What is the issue?","options":["It is too large to be a probability","It must be discrete","It fails to accumulate total probability one","It is right-continuous automatically"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aaea4038a0db08fe8b750306"}]}}